er":2,"paper_question_key":2,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":3,"t":"single_choice","p":"Eight daily maximum temperatures (°C) are: 29, 31, 28, 33, 30, 31, 27, 31. Find the mean and range.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Eight daily maximum temperatures (°C) are: 29, 31, 28, 33, 30, 31, 27, 31. Find the mean and range."}]}]},"e":"Mean = $\\frac{29+31+28+33+30+31+27+31}{8} = \\frac{240}{8} = 30\\ °\\mathrm{C}$. Range = $33 - 27 = 6\\ °\\mathrm{C}$.","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Measures of central tendency","st":"mean and range","cfg":{"question_number":3,"paper_question_key":3,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":4,"t":"single_choice","p":"Wooden rod lengths, measured to the nearest centimetre, are grouped into 20–29 cm, 30–39 cm, 40–49 cm. What are the class width and midpoint of 30–39 cm?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Wooden rod lengths, measured to the nearest centimetre, are grouped into 20–29 cm, 30–39 cm, 40–49 cm. What are the class width and midpoint of 30–39 cm?"}]}]},"e":"Measured to the nearest cm, the class 30–39 has real limits 29.5 and 39.5, so the width is 10 cm and the midpoint is 34.5 cm.","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Grouped data","st":"class width and midpoint","cfg":{"question_number":4,"paper_question_key":4,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":5,"t":"single_choice","p":"Which visual feature confirms this graph of travel times is a histogram rather than a bar graph?\n\n[Figure: Travel times to school]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which visual feature confirms this graph of travel times is a histogram rather than a bar graph?"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"histogram","title":"Travel times to school","xLabel":"Time (min)","yLabel":"Freq.","xMin":0,"xMax":40,"yMin":0,"yMax":15,"bars":[{"from":0,"to":10,"height":5},{"from":10,"to":20,"height":14},{"from":20,"to":30,"height":11},{"from":30,"to":40,"height":6}],"version":1}}}]},"e":"In a histogram the bars touch because the horizontal axis is a continuous scale; a bar graph has separated bars for distinct categories. Four bars, falling heights and a zero baseline can all occur in a bar graph too.","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Statistical graphs","st":"histogram vs bar graph","cfg":{"question_number":5,"paper_question_key":5,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":6,"t":"single_choice","p":"Fifty ping times $p$ (ms): $0 \\le p < 20$ $(f = 9)$, $20 \\le p < 40$ $(f = 19)$, $40 \\le p < 60$ $(f = 14)$, $60 \\le p < 80$ $(f = 8)$. State the modal & median classes.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fifty ping times "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"p"}},{"type":"text","text":" (ms): "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"0 \\le p < 20"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 9)"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"20 \\le p < 40"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 19)"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"40 \\le p < 60"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 14)"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"60 \\le p < 80"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 8)"}},{"type":"text","text":". State the modal & median classes."}]}]},"e":"The modal class has the highest frequency, 19: $20 \\le p < 40$. With 50 values the median lies between the 25th and 26th values; cumulative frequencies are 9 then 28, so both fall in $20 \\le p < 40$.","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Grouped data","st":"modal and median classes","cfg":{"question_number":6,"paper_question_key":6,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":7,"t":"single_choice","p":"In the box-and-whisker diagram below, what percentage of data values lie between 25 and 75?\n\n[Figure: Driver reaction data]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In the box-and-whisker diagram below, what percentage of data values lie between 25 and 75?"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"box_plot","title":"Driver reaction data","xLabel":"","xMin":0,"xMax":80,"fiveNumber":{"min":10,"q1":25,"median":38,"q3":55,"max":75},"version":1}}}]},"e":"25 is $Q_1$ and 75 is the maximum. A quarter of the data lies below $Q_1$, so the proportion from $Q_1$ up to the maximum is $100\\ \\% - 25\\ \\% = 75\\ \\%$.","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Box-and-whisker diagrams","st":"reading quartiles","cfg":{"question_number":7,"paper_question_key":7,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":8,"t":"single_choice","p":"Six sales bonuses (in 1000 FCFA) are 4, 5, 5, 6, 6, 7. When a seventh bonus of 37 is included, what happens?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Six sales bonuses (in 1000 FCFA) are 4, 5, 5, 6, 6, 7. When a seventh bonus of 37 is included, what happens?"}]}]},"e":"Six values: mean = $\\frac{33}{6} = 5.5$, median = $\\frac{5+6}{2} = 5.5$. With 37 added the mean is $\\frac{70}{7} = 10$ and the median becomes the 4th value, 6. The outlier drags the mean but barely moves the median.","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Effect of outliers","st":"outlier effect on mean and median","cfg":{"question_number":8,"paper_question_key":8,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":9,"t":"single_choice","p":"A population of $N = 5$ values $\\{4, 6, 8, 10, 12\\}$ has mean $\\mu = 8$. What is its population standard deviation $\\sigma$?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A population of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"N = 5"}},{"type":"text","text":" values "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\{4, 6, 8, 10, 12\\}"}},{"type":"text","text":" has mean "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\mu = 8"}},{"type":"text","text":". What is its population standard deviation "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sigma"}},{"type":"text","text":"?"}]}]},"e":"$\\sigma^2 = \\frac{16 + 4 + 0 + 4 + 16}{5} = \\frac{40}{5} = 8$, so $\\sigma = \\sqrt{8} = 2\\sqrt{2}$.","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Standard deviation","st":"population standard deviation","cfg":{"question_number":9,"paper_question_key":9,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":10,"t":"true_false","p":"From a grouped continuous table alone, the modal class is exact but the exact single mode is unknown.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"From a grouped continuous table alone, the modal class is exact but the exact single mode is unknown."}]}]},"e":"True: grouping keeps only class frequencies, so the modal class is known but the individual mode is not recoverable.","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Grouped data","st":"modal class of grouped data","cfg":{"question_number":10,"paper_question_key":10,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":11,"t":"true_false","p":"A stem-and-leaf diagram needs no key because stems always represent tens and leaves represent units.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A stem-and-leaf diagram needs no key because stems always represent tens and leaves represent units."}]}]},"e":"False: stems and leaves can represent any place value (for example 2 | 5 could mean 2.5 or 250), so a key is always required.","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Stem-and-leaf diagrams","st":"the key","cfg":{"question_number":11,"paper_