ange $Q_3 - Q_1$ covers the middle 50 % and ignores extreme values.","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Statistical vocabulary","st":"matching a definition to its term","cfg":{"question_number":17,"paper_question_key":17,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":18,"t":"single_choice","p":"Diagram constructed directly from the five-number summary\n\n[Figure: table]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Diagram constructed directly from the five-number summary"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["P","Q","R","S","T","U"],"rows":[["$Q_3 - Q_1$","Bar graph","$\\dfrac{\\Sigma fx}{\\Sigma f}$","Box-and-whisker","$\\dfrac{l_L + l_U}{2}$","$\\dfrac{\\Sigma (x - \\mu)^2}{N - 1}$"]],"version":1}}}]},"e":"A box-and-whisker diagram is drawn from the minimum, $Q_1$, median, $Q_3$ and maximum.","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Statistical vocabulary","st":"matching a definition to its term","cfg":{"question_number":18,"paper_question_key":18,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":19,"t":"numeric","p":"Masses of 25 avocados: $150 \\le m < 190$ g $(f = 10)$ and $190 \\le m < 230$ g $(f = 15)$. Calculate the estimated mean mass.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Masses of 25 avocados: "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"150 \\le m < 190"}},{"type":"text","text":" g "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 10)"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"190 \\le m < 230"}},{"type":"text","text":" g "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 15)"}},{"type":"text","text":". Calculate the estimated mean mass."}]}]},"e":"Midpoints are 170 g and 210 g, so the estimated mean is $\\frac{10 \\times 170 + 15 \\times 210}{25} = \\frac{4\\,850}{25} = 194$ g.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Estimated mean","st":"estimated mean from a grouped table","cfg":{"question_number":19,"paper_question_key":19,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","numeric_answer":194,"tolerance":0,"source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":20,"t":"numeric","p":"Five parcels have mean mass 14.2 kg. After one parcel is delivered, the remaining four have mean 13.5 kg. Find the delivered parcel''s mass.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Five parcels have mean mass 14.2 kg. After one parcel is delivered, the remaining four have mean 13.5 kg. Find the delivered parcel''s mass."}]}]},"e":"Total of 5 parcels $= 5 \\times 14.2 = 71$ kg; total of 4 parcels $= 4 \\times 13.5 = 54$ kg. The delivered parcel weighs $71 - 54 = 17$ kg.","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Mean","st":"finding one value from a mean","cfg":{"question_number":20,"paper_question_key":20,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":1,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","numeric_answer":17,"tolerance":0,"source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":21,"t":"single_choice","p":"Marks (out of 80) of 100 students are shown. Marks above 60 earn Distinction. Find Distinction count and $Q_1$.\n\n[Figure: Marks (out of 80) of 100 students]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Marks (out of 80) of 100 students are shown. Marks above 60 earn Distinction. Find Distinction count and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_1"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"cumulative_curve","title":"Marks (out of 80) of 100 students","xLabel":"Mark (out of 80)","yLabel":"Cum. freq.","xMin":0,"xMax":80,"yMin":0,"yMax":100,"points":[[0,0],[20,10],[30,25],[45,50],[60,80],[80,100]],"joinPoints":true,"version":1}}}]},"e":"The curve reaches cumulative frequency 80 at a mark of 60, so 100 − 80 = 20 students score above 60. $Q_1$ is at cumulative frequency 25, which the curve reaches at a mark of 30.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Cumulative frequency","st":"reading a cumulative frequency curve","cfg":{"question_number":21,"paper_question_key":21,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":22,"t":"single_choice","p":"The unequal-width histogram shows trunk diameters $d$ (cm). How many trees have $10 \\le d < 50$ cm?\n\n[Figure: Trunk diameters of trees]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The unequal-width histogram shows trunk diameters "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"d"}},{"type":"text","text":" (cm). How many trees have "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"10 \\le d < 50"}},{"type":"text","text":" cm?"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"histogram","title":"Trunk diameters of trees","xLabel":"Diameter, d (cm)","yLabel":"Freq. density","xMin":0,"xMax":50,"yMin":0,"yMax":3,"bars":[{"from":0,"to":10,"height":1.2},{"from":10,"to":25,"height":2.4},{"from":25,"to":50,"height":0.8}],"version":1}}}]},"e":"Frequency = frequency density × class width. The classes are $0 \\le d < 10$ ($1.2 \\times 10 = 12$), $10 \\le d < 25$ ($2.4 \\times 15 = 36$) and $25 \\le d < 50$ ($0.8 \\times 25 = 20$). Trees with $10 \\le d < 50$: $36 + 20 = 56$. (68 is the total of all three classes.)","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Histograms","st":"frequency density with unequal class widths","cfg":{"question_number":22,"paper_question_key":22,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":23,"t":"single_choice","p":"Classes $0 \\le x < 10$ $(f = 4)$, $10 \\le x < 20$ $(f = m)$ and $20 \\le x < 30$ $(f = 6)$ give an estimated mean of 16. Find $m$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Classes "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"0 \\le x < 10"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 4)"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"10 \\le x < 20"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = m)"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"20 \\le x < 30"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(f = 6)"}},{"type":"text","text":" give an estimated mean of 16. Find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"m"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"$\\frac{4 \\times 5 + 15m + 6 \\times 25}{m + 10} = 16 \\Rightarrow 170 + 15m = 160 + 16m \\Rightarrow m = 10$. Check: $\\frac{20 + 150 + 150}{20} = 16$.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Estimated mean","st":"finding a missing frequency from the estimated mean","cfg":{"question_number":23,"paper_question_key":23,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":24,"t":"single_choice","p":"Driver reaction times (ms) have Min = 155, $Q_1 = 210$, Med = 235, $Q_3 = 250$, Max = 305. Using fences $Q_1 - 1.5 \\times IQR$ and $Q_3 + 1.5 \\times IQR$, which is true?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Driver reaction times (ms) have Min = 155, "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_1 = 210"}},{"type":"text","text":", Med = 235, "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_3 = 250"}},{"type":"text","text":", Max = 305. Using fences "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_1 - 1.5 \\times IQR"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_3 + 1.5 \\times IQR"}},{"type":"text","text":", which is true?"}]}]},"e":"$IQR = 250 - 210 = 40$, so $1.5 \\times IQR = 60$ and the fences are $210 - 60 = 150$ and $250 + 60 = 310$. The minimum 155 and maximum 305 both lie inside the fences, so there are no outliers.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Box-and-whisker diagrams","st":"outlier fences","cfg":{"question_number":24,"paper_question_key":24,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":25,"t":"single_choice","p":"A population of 18 masses has $\\mu = 48$ g and $\\sigma = 6$ g. Each mass $x$ is transformed via $y = \\frac{1}{3}x + 8$. Find $\\mu_y$ and $\\sigma_y$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A population of 18 masses has "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\mu = 48"}},{"type":"text","text":" g and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sigma = 6"}},{"type":"text","text":" g. Each mass "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"x"}},{"type":"text","text":" is transformed via "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"y = \\frac{1}{3}x + 8"}},{"type":"text","text":". Find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\mu_y"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sigma_y"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"Under $y = ax + b$, $\\mu_y = a\\mu + b = \\frac{1}{3}(48) + 8 = 24$ g and $\\sigma_y = |a|\\sigma = \\frac{1}{3}(6) = 2$ g. Adding 8 shifts the mean but does not change the spread.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Linear coding","st":"effect of a linear transform on mean and standard deviation","cfg":{"question_number":25,"paper_question_key":25,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"challenge","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":26,"t":"open_ended","p":"Data classification & graph selection [3 marks] Classify each variable as qualitative, quantitative discrete, or quantitative continuous, and name a suitable chart (bar graph or histogram) for (b) and (c):\n(a) Blood group (A, B, AB, O) of hospital blood donors. [1]\n(b) Number of solar panels installed per school roof in a district. [1]\n(c) Exact mass (in grams) of 120 laboratory soil samples. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Data classification & graph selection [3 marks] Classify each variable as qualitative, quantitative discrete, or quantitative continuous, and name a suitable chart (bar graph or histogram) for (b) and (c):"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Blood group (A, B, AB, O) of hospital blood donors. [1]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Number of solar panels installed per school roof in a district. [1]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(c) Exact mass (in grams) of 120 laboratory soil samples. [1]"}]}]},"e":"","m":3,"df":"easy","tp":"Data classification","st":"choosing a suitable chart","cfg":{"question_number":26,"paper_question_key":26,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":1,"focus":"Blood group"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Solar panels"},{"label":"(c)","marks":1,"focus":"Soil-sample mass"}],"response_guidance":"Classify each variable and name a suitable chart for (b) and (c).","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":27,"t":"open_ended","p":"Central tendency & dispersion [3 marks] Student absences recorded per day over 11 school days: 3, 1, 4, 0, 2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2, 0.\n(a) Find the mode, median, and range of daily absences. [2]\n(b) Calculate the exact mean number of absences per day. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Central tendency & dispersion [3 marks] Student absences recorded per day over 11 school days: 3, 1, 4, 0, 2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2, 0."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Find the mode, median, and range of daily absences. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Calculate the exact mean number of absences per day. [1]"}]}]},"e":"","m":3,"df":"easy","tp":"Measures of central tendency","st":"mode, median, range and mean","cfg":{"question_number":27,"paper_question_key":27,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"basic","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Mode, median, range"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"Exact mean"}],"response_guidance":"Show your ordering and every calculation.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table","statistics"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table","statistics"],"source":"myp4-term1-summative-b-2026"}},{"n":28,"t":"open_ended","p":"Grouped discrete-limit distribution [4 marks] Lengths (to the nearest mm) of 40 beetle specimens:\n\n[Figure: table]\n(a) State the lower & upper class boundaries of 20–24, the modal class, and the class containing the median. [2]\n(b) Using exact mid-interval values, calculate an estimate of the mean length. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Grouped discrete-limit distribution [4 marks] Lengths (to the nearest mm) of 40 beetle specimens:"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["Length (mm)","10–14","15–19","20–24","25–29","30–34"],"rows":[["Freq. (f)","5","13","12","7","3"]],"version":1}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the lower & upper class boundaries of 20–24, the modal class, and the class containing the median. [2]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(b) Using exact mid-interval values, calculate an estimate of the mean length. [2]"}]}]},"e":"","m":4,"df":"medium","tp":"Grouped data","st":"discrete limits and estimated mean","cfg":{"question_number":28,"paper_question_key":28,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowin